\description{
The Drift Diffusion Model (DDM) describes a two-choice decision as noisy
evidence accumulating between two boundaries until one of them is reached.
The boundary reached is the choice and the time taken is the decision time.
The observed RT is that decision time shifted by a non-decision time \code{ndt},
and a fixed proportion \code{poutlier} of responses is generated by an outlier
process instead of by the diffusion.

Functions:
\itemize{
\item \code{rcogmod_ddm()}: Simulates random draws from the DDM.
\item \code{dcogmod_ddm()}: Computes the density (likelihood).
\item \code{cogmod_ddm()}: Creates a \code{brms::custom_family()} for use in \code{brms} models.
\item \code{cogmod_ddm_stanvars()}: Generates the \code{stanvars} to pass to \code{brm()}.
\item \code{p_outlier()}: Per-trial posterior probability of being an outlier.
}
}
\section{Response coding}{
The response coded \code{1} (\code{response} here, \code{dec()} in a \code{brms} formula) is the
\strong{upper} boundary and the response coded \code{0} is the \strong{lower} one, following
\code{brms}'s own \code{wiener()} family. Two consequences are worth keeping in mind
when reading a fitted model:
\itemize{
\item \code{bias} is measured \emph{from the lower boundary}, so \code{bias > 0.5} places the
starting point closer to the response coded \code{1}, and \code{bias < 0.5} closer to
the response coded \code{0}. Since first-passage times are shorter for the nearer
boundary, \code{bias > 0.5} makes responses coded \code{1} \emph{faster} than responses
coded \code{0}, and \code{bias < 0.5} makes them slower. At exactly \code{bias = 0.5} the
two conditional RT distributions are identical, whatever the drift rate.
\item A positive \code{drift} pushes the accumulator towards the response coded \code{1}.
When \code{0} codes correct responses and \code{1} codes errors (a common choice when
the task has no natural stimulus-to-boundary mapping), good performance
therefore corresponds to a \emph{negative} drift rate.
}
}

\section{Parameterization}{
\code{ndt} is expressed \strong{directly, in seconds} (through a log link in the \code{brms}
family). Nothing about it is taken from the data: it is not bounded by the
fastest observed response, so a non-decision time that varies by condition or
by participant can exceed the sample minimum wherever the data support it.

This replaces the earlier \code{tau} / \code{minrt} pair, in which \code{ndt = tau * minrt}
with \code{minrt} set to the fastest observed RT. That capped the non-decision
time at an order statistic of the sample, so any condition or participant
whose true \code{ndt} exceeded the fastest observed response was inexpressible,
and the misfit surfaced as spurious effects on the other parameters.

\code{sigmatau} went with it, and is now \strong{\code{sigmandt}}. It is the between-trial
\emph{range} of the non-decision time (\code{st0} in the usual notation), expressed
directly in the same unit as the data rather than as a fraction of \code{minrt},
with \code{ndt} the lower bound of the resulting Uniform. The old name was a
compound of a parameter that no longer exists and a scale factor the user no
longer sees.
}

\section{Between-trial variability}{
\code{sigmadrift}, \code{sigmabias} and \code{sigmandt} extend the classic 4-parameter
process to the full 7-parameter one. Each is legitimately \strong{zero}, and
setting all three to zero in the formula recovers the classic model:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, sigmadrift = 0, sigmabias = 0,
         sigmandt = 0, ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1, family = cogmod_ddm())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Writing \code{sigmadrift = 0} \strong{fixes} the parameter; leaving it out of \code{bf()}
altogether \emph{estimates} it, which is not the same thing. All three are hard to
recover even from a lot of data, and each has a flat direction at its own
floor - the link only reaches zero at minus infinity, and the likelihood stops
changing well before then - so \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} gives all three deliberately
tight priors. Fixing the ones a design cannot identify is usually better than
estimating them behind a prior.
}

\section{The outlier component}{
A shifted distribution assigns exactly zero density to any response faster
than \code{ndt}, which puts a hard boundary in the likelihood at the fastest
observed RT. Mixing in a component with support over the whole positive line
removes it: every response keeps positive density whatever \code{ndt} is, so the
boundary becomes a finite cost rather than a wall and the log-density stays
smooth and differentiable.

Because this model produces a \strong{choice as well as a time}, the contaminant
has to produce both. It is a guess: the choice is uniform over the two
options, and the RT is a \strong{half Normal} with scale \code{0.2} seconds.

\deqn{f(t, k) = p \frac{1}{K} g(t) + (1 - p) f_k(t - ndt)}

The \code{1 / K} is what keeps the total summing to one over the response options;
without it it would come to \code{1 + poutlier}.

\code{poutlier} is a \emph{rate}, not a classification: the model never labels
individual trials, it estimates what share of them came from elsewhere. Use
\code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} for per-trial posterior probabilities.
}

\section{Reaction times must be in seconds}{
The outlier component's scale is a constant in seconds, and so are the
priors \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} supplies. There is no argument for changing the
unit: the \code{minrt} argument that used to rescale the component was removed in
0.2.1. Millisecond data fails silently rather than loudly - the outlier
component contributes nothing and the min-RT boundary comes back. See the
corresponding section of \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} for the full account, which
applies unchanged here.
}

\section{Implementation}{
The 4-parameter process is simulated and evaluated with
\code{\link[brms:Wiener]{brms::rwiener()}}/\code{\link[brms:Wiener]{brms::dwiener()}} (which require the \code{RWiener} package).
The full 7-parameter model is built on top of these rather than delegated to
another package: between-trial variability is simulated by drawing the
per-trial parameters, and evaluated by combining a closed-form drift
correction with Gauss-Legendre quadrature over the starting point and
non-decision time.

In Stan the decision component is \code{wiener_lpdf()}, called with its own
non-decision time set to zero because the shift is applied by the mixture
around it, and short-circuited to the cheaper 4- and 5-parameter forms
whenever the start-point and non-decision-time ranges both vanish. The
cheapest of the three, Stan's classic 4-parameter density, is used only where
it is sound: it returns \code{-inf} with \strong{NaN} derivatives once the rescaled
decision time \code{t / boundary^2} falls below about \code{6.6e-4}, or once the density
underflows, and a NaN derivative on a trial the mixture gives no weight to
still turns the gradient of the whole model to NaN. Those calls go to the
\code{sv}-capable form instead, which the two agree with to \code{1e-13} where they
meet.
}

\section{Fitting}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, boundary ~ Condition, bias ~ 1,
              sigmadrift = 0, sigmabias = 0, sigmandt = 0,
              ndt ~ 1, poutlier ~ 1, family = cogmod_ddm())
brms::brm(f, data = df,
          prior    = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          init     = cogmod_inits(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Use \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}} rather than \code{init = 0}. \code{brms} initialises on the
unconstrained scale, so \code{init = 0} puts \code{ndt} at \code{exp(0) = 1} second - above
nearly every sub-second RT, which leaves every response attributed to the
outlier component and the diffusion parameters with no gradient at all.
}

\section{Predictions exclude the outlier component}{
\code{posterior_predict()} and \code{posterior_epred()} describe the \strong{diffusion
alone} by default, as if \code{poutlier} were zero, because the outlier component
is a fixed regularizer rather than a claim about how guesses are distributed.
Use \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}} for the fitted mixture - chiefly for
\code{brms::pp_check()} - and \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}} to go back. \code{log_lik()} is
always the full mixture.
}

